+
+The following parameters exist only if the encoder device has the controls for
+them, and are static:
+
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_BITRATE_MODE* offers the modes in the device's
+*V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_BITRATE_MODE* menu. The constant quality mode
+(*C2Config::BITRATE_IGNORE*) takes its quality, 1 to 100, from
+*C2_PARAMKEY_QUALITY* and can be changed at run-time.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_BITRATE* is limited to the range of the device's bitrate control.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_PICTURE_QUANTIZATION* sets the lowest and highest quantization
+parameter, the tightest of the bounds for any picture type, within the range of
+the device's minimum and maximum QP controls.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_GOP* with a B-frame layer sets the largest run of B-frames, up
+to the device's *V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_B_FRAMES* maximum, and not for H.264
+Baseline. The encoder then returns its output in the order of the stream, and
+*C2_PARAMKEY_INPUT_DELAY* follows the run. *C2_PARAMKEY_PIPELINE_DELAY*, which
+the framework reports as the latency of the encoder, is one frame longer: the
+frames of a run wait for the reference frame after them, and a client that
+waits for an output sooner stalls the stream.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_TEMPORAL_LAYERING* sets layers of P frames, up to what the
+device's hierarchical coding controls allow. Layers turn B-frames off and
+constant bitrate into variable bitrate. The bitrate ratio of the base layer
+splits the bitrate between the two layers.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_COLOR_ASPECTS* sets the colorimetry of the raw frames, and
+*C2_PARAMKEY_VUI_COLOR_ASPECTS* reports what the stream carries.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_HDR_STATIC_INFO* sets the HDR10 mastering display and content
+light level of an HEVC Main 10 stream, if the device has those controls and
+takes P010 frames. A device without P010 input does not offer Main 10.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_PIXEL_FORMAT* lists P010 beside the flexible YUV 4:2:0 format
+when the encoder offers Main 10, so the framework lists *COLOR_FormatYUVP010*.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_PROFILE_LEVEL* of the output reports the level the device chose
+for the stream, read from the first coded headers, when it differs from the
+ceiling the client asked for.
 
 The following parameters are dynamic, and can be freely adjusted at run-time:
 
@@ -326,16 +367,48 @@ framerate.
 - *C2_PARAMKEY_REQUEST_SYNC_FRAME*: This parameter can be used to request
 additional key frames in addition to the periodic ones requested through the
 *C2_PARAMKEY_SYNC_FRAME_INTERVAL* parameter.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_OPERATING_RATE* and *C2_PARAMKEY_PRIORITY*: The rate at which the
+client gives frames, and whether it needs them coded at that rate (priority 0)
+or as the device finds the time (a priority below 0). They change the system
+resources the instance needs, see below.
+
+### System Resources:
+
+The component store reports the capacity of the device, and each encoder the
+resources it needs, so that Android can tell what is left
+(*MediaCodec.getGloballyAvailableResources()*). Both have two vendor ids:
+
+- Codec sessions (*C2PlatformConfig::VENDOR_START*): the capacity is the sum of
+the concurrent instance bounds of the codecs the store offers, and every
+instance needs one.
+- Pixel rate (*VENDOR_START* + 1): the capacity is
+*ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.video_pixel_rate*. An instance needs the width times the
+height times the frame rate (the operating rate, if it is set) of its stream
+when it runs in real time, and nothing when it does not.
+
+An encoder reports a change of its need with its next output. A start that
+would take the pixel rate of all instances past the capacity fails with
+*C2_NO_MEMORY*, which Android raises as *ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_RESOURCE*; the first
+instance is always admitted, as the device serves one of whatever size it
+supports. A decoder takes part by reporting *videoResourcesNeeded()* and
+claiming its pixel rate from *VideoPixelRateBudget* (*VideoResources.h*).
 
 ### Supported Input Pixel Formats:
 
-The V4L2 encoder supports various input pixel formats, however frames are
-currently always passed to the V4L2 encoder in the NV12 format. If a video frame
+The V4L2 encoder supports various input pixel formats. Frames are passed to the
+V4L2 encoder in the NV12 format, or in P010 for HEVC Main 10. If a video frame
 using a different pixel format is passed to the encoder, format conversion will
-be performed to convert the frame to the NV12 format.
+be performed to convert the frame to the NV12 format, except for packed RGB
+frames (RGBA_8888 and RGBX_8888 blocks) that the device takes as they are: the
+encoder then asks the device for the format of the first frame before it
+queues it.
 
 ### Additional Features:
 
+A device that holds the bitrate by leaving frames out returns the output buffer
+of such a frame with the error flag and no data. The work item of the frame
+finishes without an output, and the items behind it are not held up.
+
 To improve the resilience of H.264 video streams when data is missing, SPS and
